priate analyte panels  for example include cytokine storms  immune activation as well as liver and  kidney injury  the last category branches into the  areas of exploratory biomarker  identification  and patient stratification where we  profile biobank samples  including those from internal clinical  trials  as well as various disease population  samples that we typically source  with the help from our academic  collaboration partners  including the corresponding normal  healthy volunteer controls  in order to be able to support this wide  range of study types  we need a very broad coverage of analyte  panels for the different purposes and  therapeutic areas  we currently have over 600 analytes in  active use  around 60 percent are from a variety of  milliporous milliplex panels  we also have an ever growing list of new  analytes in development  which can either mean that we are  working to miniaturize  existing panels to etcher our  capabilities or develop completely  new analytes mostly through very  fruitful collaboration with the milipoi  team  our different panels are broadly  classified into  innate and adaptive immunity heart  failure fibrosis  and toxicity the immunology and the  soluble checkpoint panels are the ones i  will be focusing on for the remainder of  the webinar  with a small excursion into our  cardiovascular and heart failure analyte  applications  i had mentioned previously that a lot of  our remit and biomarker profiling at bms  is on high throughput and as such we  have optimized the workflow to be able  to handle  up to 2 000 samples every week with  sample requirements  as low as 5 microliters per panel  a large fraction of the sample types we  deal with are serum  plasma or supernatant or lysate from a  cell culture or tissue  we do however also work with more exotic  matrices  such as azides blister fluid synovial  fluid  bronchoalveolar velavage or  increasingly urine we have been building  out this capability in the last few  years and its use across the bms  research community has  really taken off to date we have  profiled more than 250  000 samples for more than 170 different  programs  which has resulted in more than 11  million data points  that brings its own challenges and data  management  which is why the custom in-house i.t  platform  i mentioned previously is of crucial  importance  now i want to switch gears a little bit  and focus  specifically on our immuno oncology  support  of the roughly 47 000 samples we  analyzed last year almost half came from  io  as you can see in the breakdown the  majority of samples are from human  origin  followed by mouse and on average  each individual study has 70 samples  but the range per study is very wide  from as little as  10 to as many as 800 individual samples  similarly we profile a mean of 41  cytokines per sample across a range from  14  which would be a single panel up all the  way to 98  and multiple panels being run for  broader profiling  the average number of panels we run per  sample is  three we have certainly seen a demand  for more deep profiling especially for  pressure sample  in line with more and more analytes  covering  different biological pathways becoming  available  now you might ask yourself why we expend  such a large  effort to investigate soluble biomarkers  in general  and cytokines in particular for  immuno-oncology research  it might become clearer when looking at  cancer as essentially the result of a  dysregulated immune system  as in ira mellman's and then chance  cancer immunity cycle  where every step of successful tumor  eradication  is dependent on cytokines as master  regulators  from tumor antigen presentation to the  activation and priming of relevant  immune cells  trafficking of those cells to the site  of malignancy and finally  infiltration recognition and destruction  of the tumor cells  we have long known that cytokines play  doer roles they contribute to tumor  genesis  for example by promoting pathogenic  inflammation  and tissue damage or invasion metastases  and angiogenesis  but also play key roles in preventing  tumor formation  or resolving early stage malignancies  understanding this intricate balance of  the immune system's master regulators  is key in developing successful i o  drugs  and the first step is understanding  which molecules are present  at what stage of cancer development  cytokines themselves are in fact also  interesting therapeutic options for  cancer treatment  this graphic from a 2019 review shows  which ones have  been or are currently being investigated  in clinical trials  from interference to gmcsf and various  interleukins  interferon and il2 are approved  treatments for hairy cell leukemia and  melanoma and renal cell  cancer respectively both mechanisms of  action rely on broad  immune stimulation  with this outsized role of soluble  biomarkers  including cytokines chemokines and  soluble checkpoint molecules  it is probably not surprising that we  have a large selection of analytes in  routine use for io focus  projects here i am showing the 133  analytes and their corresponding panels  that we use for human sample profiling  we have similar if somewhat smaller  selections we use for mouse and sinnoh  studies  all these panels i'm showing here are  based off  existing milliplex panels and i want to  use the next couple slides  to show some of our experiences working  with those reagents  as we are running so many different  samples and analytes  we need very robust kits and reagents  generally we require strong performance  when it comes to reproducibility and  precision  sensitivity and specificity  reproducibility between  assays but also between different lots  of kids  is key as we support multiple  longitudinal studies where samples can  be received  months or years apart in addition a good  performance in our custom  3d4 well format is essential this is  something we will extensively test when  new multiplexes come on the market  that we want to add to our platform  additionally  we work with an internal team of  statisticians  to ensure a robust qc process for all  runs  and reliably determine the lower and  upper limits of detection and  quantification  we have a database that keeps track of  the key performance indicators for each  essay run that will automatically flag  if data  falls outside of the pre-specified  acceptance criteria  for the aspects of sensitivity and  specificity  i will share some observations in the  following slides  sensitivity and in particular the lower  limit of quantification  are critical aspects for a lot of  cytokine biomarkers  as a large fraction of those proteins  are low abundance  particularly when using unstimulated  samples or healthy controls  we have frequently struggled with  detecting certain biomarkers reliably in  different sample types  let alone properly quantify them that's  why we are always interested in new high  sensitivity kits  the data on this slide shows our  experience with the high sensitivity  milliplex panels  for human and mouse we are comparing the  lloq  the lower limit of quantification  between the original panel  and the high sensitivity version note  that  lloq values are determined from the  performance and fit of the standard  curve  under our specific 384 well assay  conditions  and our protocol differs from what comes  of the kit  so your individual mileage may vary  as you can see most of the analytes have  an improved lloq  in some cases very significantly by  50 or more fold some analytes for this  has been especially helpful  are il-10 il-12p70  il-4 il5 and il-6  for our customers we generally recommend  the high sensitivity panels as the  default choice when multiple panel  versions are available  and aim to move as many analytes into  those types of multiplexes as possible  next i want to give a little insight  into specificity  using the soluble checkpoint panels  which are of key importance for io drug  research  especially when it comes to patient  stratification  the data on this slide refers to the 17  plaits checkpoint panel one  that we adopted a couple of years ago  when we initially investigated available  panels in the market  we wanted to get a sense for how they  performed against qualified recombinant  proteins  owing to our broad project portfolio we  had a variety of in-house produced  recombinant proteins available  that we used for this purpose the heat  map on this slide  shows the fluorescence intensity values  across the 17 plaques on the x-axis  versus the kit's standards and our  spiked in control proteins  pd1 pdlt1 pdl2  and idle on the y-axis across the top  you can see  that each standard produces a nice  dose-dependent signal in its respective  channel  going down the list you'll notice that  in our hands the specificity of the kit  is excellent  each recombinant protein only produces a  signal in the channel  or with the bead we would expect it in  and everything else looks like  background  at the time we were also evaluating  other kits  and i'm showing the example that didn't  quite behave  as well in this slide same concept as  before  slightly different analyte composition  the kit standards once again behave well  and the control proteins produce strong  signals with their respective beats  as you want to see however unnarrower  assay conditions  with this kit we also observed  significant cross reactivity with  several beads  which means a signal is showing up in a  channel  where you would not expect it see the  blue errors  which ultimately means that we couldn't  be confident this particular multiplex  would faithfully detect the independent  checkpoint analytes in our patient  samples  next i want to share with you some very  exciting data from a new 31 plex  the milliport checkpoint panel 2 where  we were involved in the beta test  process  this panel is an exciting extension as  it contains a broad variety of  additional checkpoints and ligands  such as members of the b7 family and  many of the analytes are complementary  to checkpoint panel one  as before in this beta test we ran a  variety of recombinant proteins against  the multiplex  and see very good overall specificity  we have since begun testing this panel  on real world samples  and see similar encouraging data i  believe this panel has now also  launched and reagents are commercially  available so we are working to add it to  our platform for routine application  now for the next section of my talk i  want to focus on some key applications  for biomarker profiling in  our io discovery research and share some  case studies to give you a flavor of how  we use the data to progress projects  the case studies encompass three  different flavors of experiments  first are in vitro or ex-vivo cell  models  such as profiling the biomarker  signatures of primary  immune or tumor cells as well as core  cultures  we use these experiments typically early  in a project during the target  validation phase  the second type of experiments are in  the pre-clinical  or in vivo group where we profile tumor  models and animals  either in vivo or x-vivo and also  analyze samples from exploratory talk  studies  often performed in cinemalgous monkeys  for a particular  drop candidate the third category  encompasses all types of patient samples  these are typically the samples that are  the most precious  and where we perform the broadest  possible analysis  in the early stages of an io discovery  project  we use a number of complex cellular  models  to profile a particular targets or  compounds behavior  one of those models is the culture of  genetically engineered primary t  cells which express a known  antigen-specific t-cell receptor  with matched tumor cells presenting the  cognate antigen  on their mhc molecules we can measure  tumor cell killing as shown  in the pictures on the right in the  absence of antigen recognition  the tumor cells in