d some crispr  knockouts of  key proteins in those pathways the first  one  is a better 2mko better 2m  is an essential component of the mhc  complex on the cell surface  so in the absence of this protein no  functional mhc  complex can be formed and the cell is  not capable of presenting  any antigen irrespective of whether they  are derived from inside or outside of  the cell  the second knockout is the tap 1ko  tab 1 is an essential transporter for  intracellular antigen processing and  loading  so in the absence of this protein the  cells are expected  not to be able to present newly  expressed neoantigens  but are anticipated to have functional  mhc  complexes on the cell surface indeed our  cytokine data using the antigen-loaded  controls  confirm these expectations the tumor  cell without a functional mhc  cannot induce t cell activation even in  the presence of the antigen  whereas the tap1ko that still has the  mhc  complex is performing like the wild type  in both cases our epigenetic modulators  don't work anymore  due to the deficiencies in the  intracellular antigen presentation  pathway  which together with other data not shown  here  gave us confidence that the approach of  epigenetic modulation  is promising  my next case study comes from a mouse  tumor model  specifically an orthotopic transplant  model  of pancreatic cancer that was studied in  collaborations with a group at johns  hopkins  the specific question in the study was  if radiation therapy  a standard of care approach can enhance  immunotherapy efficacy of pd-1  inhibitors  or combinations of pd-1 and idle  inhibitors  ultimately the data revealed that  survival was improved  by a combination of radiation and ntpd-1  but that idle combinations were not  useful  and that this treatment effect  correlated with a systemic interference  gamma response as shown by multiplex  cytokine profiling  of mouse plasma samples  now i want to give you a flavor for how  we use cytokine profiling to evaluate  the toxicity risks of our later stage  molecules  before filing an ind in this particular  example  a bms molecule was given in increasing  doses  group one is the control group group two  through four  received the drug dry cevic apart  we received serum samples for cytokine  profiling  at different time points from just  before the first  dose until about two weeks after the  last dose  we assessed a total of 44 different  cinemalgous analytes in two  panels i have chosen an example of five  relevant ones for the line graphs  where each line represents an individual  monkey  we observed a dose-dependent transient  increase in inflammatory cytokines  that peaks three to six hours after  dosing  and returns to baseline within about 24  hours  we also saw that the second dose did not  cause the same magnitude of effect  together with other data we could  conclude  that the molecule was well tolerated and  did not  cause a cytokine storm in the animals  which is a concern for the particular  mechanism of action  we are targeting with this drug based on  other in vitro data  generated over the course of this  project we could also conclude that the  observed cytokine induction  is largely on target and on mechanism  in my last io specific case study i want  to talk very  briefly about our work with patient  samples  when we obtain these types of samples we  typically perform broad profiling  as much as the sample volumes permit  often for 100 plus analytes  this is also where we mostly use the  checkpoint panels  as exemplified in the heat map which  aggregates several hundred  serum samples by tumor type and for  which we can see  clear differences in checkpoint  molecules detected  ultimately the goal is to build a  sufficiently large database that allows  to identify  predictive biomarker signatures when  linked to patient phenotypic or  treatment data another focus for us is  performing longitudinal studies  on treatment particularly with anti-peo1  therapies  to investigate response and resistance  mechanism  and their impact on biomarker signatures  further we also use tumor explants to  evaluate individual treatments and  treatment combos  and the biomarker data is typically a  key readout in those studies  with that i want to take a little  excursion into the realm of heart  failure biomarkers  and share with you a study we  contributed to  that had considerable translational  impact  heart failure in particular the subtype  with preserved  ejection fraction also known as hepath  where the left ventricle is not able to  fill with blood properly  during the diastolic filling phase but  otherwise  appears to pump normally represents an  increasing burden  on public health especially in aging  population  it has despite many many attempts  no proven pharmacological interventions  and it's  five year survival after a heart failure  hospitalization event  is only 35 worse than  several types of tumors it is being  increasingly recognized as a very  heterogeneous disease  that it has in common with cancer  noticing biomarkers exist  and remedying that was the objective of  this project  we contributed profiling data for the  samples for this effort  were sourced from a biobank of the  topcat clinical trial  that investigated the use of the  aldosterone  antagonist spironolactone in half-path  patients  older than 50 in an international trial  we obtained these samples for a very  active collaboration with upenn  ultimately this trial like many others  before it  failed to meet its end point which was a  composite of  deaf aborted cardiac arrest and heart  failure related hospital admission  we profile those samples using a custom  bms  cardiovascular panel that includes 50  analytes  covering a multitude of biological  processes  from inflammation to angiogenesis  fibrosis  cardiomyocyte and adipocyte biology  and more  in our analyses we found that the  peripheral biomarker data  together with the patient metadata  helped stratify the trial population  into three distinct subgroups  key circulating biomarker cardiac and  vascular features  were found indicating the staying  patterns  the phenyl groups exhibited different  prognoses  and differential responses to  spironolactone  the first group was slightly younger  with milder symptoms  a high likelihood of smoking but low  levels of naturoretic peptides  which are usually associated with worse  outcomes  the second group screwed older and  female  had more chronic kidney disease and  atrial fibrillation  and a distinct signature of active  mineral metabolism and inflammation  the third group was characterized by  high levels of obesity  diabetes and depression and presented  high biomarker levels for inflammation  especially with regards to tnf alpha  fibrosis and angiogenesis  this group failed the worst while the  overall top card clinical trial failed  in fetal group 3 there were signals of a  treatment benefit  in this retrospective analysis the study  highlights the power of biomarker data  stratifying patient populations  in highly heterogeneous diseases to  identify those subsets  that have the highest likelihood of  benefiting from a particular treatment  approach  with the same biomarker data our upend  collaborators around julio chirinos  build a machine learning model with the  goal of identifying  predictive signatures indeed when  looking at the entirety of  measured markers versus the patient  metadata and primary study endpoint  several markers appeared to be  significantly associated and clusters  emerged for inflammatory  metabolism fibrosis and angiogenesis  pathways  as i mentioned before half pep doesn't  have  any well-defined biomarker signatures  but something called the magic risk  score  is frequently used to evaluate risks for  patients to die  of heart failure at one and three years  after diagnosis  this score incorporates a number of  clinical parameters  such as age weight whether someone has  diabetes  copd takes certain medicines and several  other factors  the utility of the score varies by  patient population  when using the magic risk score to look  at the top cap patient population  its predictiveness is pretty weak as  indicated by a c  index of just over 0.6 a c  index of 0.5 means the same predictive  value as random chance  whereas a number of 1 equates to a  perfect prediction  the biomarker machine learning model  performs  much better than the magic score with a  c index greater than 0.7  in the top cut patient population  interestingly  this evaluation also was true in a  completely independent cohort  the pen heart failure study so  essentially the combination of  prevalent biomarkers in the patient's  periphery  could predict if this patient would  experience a heart failure related  incident such as hospitalization or  death  this once sufficiently validated could  eventually be a new and easy way for  classifying patients into risk groups  from a single blood draw overall  this work has presented tantalizing  evidence that a plasma  biomarker signature for half path is  achievable  and may outperform current methods for  assessing risk  or can be effectively combined with them  bms and upend are now part of a  worldwide heart failure consortium  that aims to validate those findings in  13 international cohorts  with more than 5 000 patients  i hope i was able to give you a flavor  for how we use  high throughput multiplex biomarker  profiling and bms  and want to finish with an outlook on  our current and future focus areas  an ongoing effort is in the expansion of  coverage  adding new panels and analytes to the  platform  or developing new analytes altogether in  close collaboration with manipur  we also have a growing need for custom  disease-specific panels  to support targeted biomarker studies  we also try and consolidate and merge  panels wherever possible to reduce  sample volume needs  and overall workload we also spend a lot  of effort into closely related areas  data analytics and data integration i  have shown you that we profile tens of  thousands of samples a year  across a wide variety of experimental  designs  with that comes a strong need for a data  and also very importantly a metadata  infrastructure  to facilitate the widest possible use of  the results  so we constantly try and optimize our  data registration  and database methods this is also  key in facilitating enhanced  bioinformatics methods for data analyses  as well as efforts to implement machine  learning  particularly for generating disease  predictive biomarker signatures  the heart failure biomarker study i  showed you  is a prime example of why these methods  are of value  biomarker data also becomes much more  informative when it can be overlaid with  other data types generated from the same  sample  such as sequencing of phenotyping for  example  lastly an effort that we have ongoing  focuses on new technologies that allow  investigating the cytokine profile of a  single cell  as we know immune cells in particular  show very heterogeneous responses to  treatment  and marry it to the bulk biomarker data  that we routinely produce from luminex  profiling  finally i want to thank you for your  attention and  acknowledge the bms cytokine team in  particular  melissa yard and nathan cheadle who are  the engine of this platform and keep the  samples and data flowing on a day-by-day  basis  we are also indebted to all our bms  collaborators  both those who enable us to physically  run all the instruments we need  as well as our partners in the  individual therapeutic areas  i also want to acknowledge our strong  partners at millipore  particularly jan and chain who we meet  with frequently  in non-pademic times as well as brook  who supports our ever expanding list of  requests for new analytes  lastly i want to express my gratitude to  our upend collaborators  whose support is invaluable in the  cardiovascular and heart fail